WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.849 --> 00:00:06.920
Linear Algebra courses often include a part
about changing the basis of a vector or a

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:11.420
matrix, and in my experience, this part of
the course can be a bit confusing- especially

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:16.250
this mysterious change of basis formula they
give you. What you’ll see in this video

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:22.039
is, if you really understand what changing
a basis actually is, how to do it will become

00:00:22.039 --> 00:00:31.290
obvious to you, and you won’t really need
the formula after all.

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:38.519
So here’s what change of basis is. Imagine
Alice is trying to describe a vector to Bob.

00:00:38.519 --> 00:00:44.269
The way to describe any vector is to pick
a basis, then the vector can be described

00:00:44.269 --> 00:00:51.030
as a linear combination of those basis vectors.
She can write this compactly by writing it

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:57.729
as a column like this... and she can tell
Bob that column is. But there’s a problem.

00:00:57.729 --> 00:01:04.060
Bob isn’t using the same basis vectors as
her. He’s using this basis instead. And

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:12.290
so to him, this column vector means this sum,
which is this vector- which is completely

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:19.430
different from the one Alice has in mind.
Alice realises that, for Bob to understand

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:27.130
what vector she meant, she needs to describe
her vector in his basis for him. That’s

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:33.630
what change of basis is. Taking a vector written
in one basis and writing it in another basis.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:39.049
Let’s see how we can do this in a simple
example first. Pretend that Bob’s basis

00:01:39.049 --> 00:01:46.590
is this one, and Alice’s basis is exactly
the same, but rotated by 45 degrees. Say the

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:52.799
vector that Alice wants to communicate to
Bob is her second basis vector, this one here.

00:01:52.799 --> 00:01:57.939
She knows exactly what Bob’s basis vectors
are, and now she wants to write her vector

00:01:57.939 --> 00:02:06.340
in his basis. As in, she wants to write that
a2 equals something times b1 plus something

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:13.400
times b2. What should these numbers be? Here
are 4 different options, choose which it is,

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:18.530
and put the answer in the poll in the corner.
Don't worry, you don’t need to do any calculation

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:23.060
to figure it out, you should be able to work
it out from where the minus signs are. Pause

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:34.970
the video now to think about it.

00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:43.190
The answer is 2), b1 -b2 divided by square
root 2. This is because if you turn b2 around

00:02:43.190 --> 00:02:49.580
to get -b2, then add these two vectors, you
do get something in the same direction as

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:56.430
a2, but it needs to be shorted. If you weren’t
sure how to approach that question, may I

00:02:56.430 --> 00:03:01.840
suggest my video on vectors as a refresher?
The link is in the description and in the

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:05.680
corner.
Now say that Alice wanted to write her first

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:17.170
vector in Bob’s basis. Which one of these
options would that be? Hopefully you can see

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:21.250
it’s 1.

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:29.420
Ok, so Alice now knows how to write these
two basis vectors in Bob’s language. But

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:35.520
what if she wants to send some other vector?
Well now she basically can! Why? Imagine she

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:41.600
writes her vector as this column of numbers.
That means the vector is this linear combo

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:48.950
of her basis vectors. She needs to translate
this whole thing to Bob’s basis. But she

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:56.530
already knows how to translate her own basis
vectors. After doing that, she’s done right?

00:03:56.530 --> 00:04:01.880
Because the resulting column in Bob’s basis
now.

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:06.760
What we’ve learnt from this example is,
no matter what kind of vector Alice has and

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:11.790
what basis she’s written it in, as long
as Alice somehow figures out how to write

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:18.440
each one of her basis vectors in Bob’s basis,
that’s enough to translate her vector to

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:22.290
his basis.

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:26.510
We could leave this here and be happy, but
there’s a really convenient way for Alice

00:04:26.510 --> 00:04:33.250
to do all this translating. We could represent
it as a matrix. As in, Alice just puts in

00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:39.240
her vector in her basis and the matrix spits
out what it should be in Bob’s. We’re

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:44.240
going to call this the change of basis matrix.

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:49.600
Going back to our example, Alice knows that
her first basis vector is written like this

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:55.240
in Bob’s basis. From this fact alone, and
not from any calculations, work out what the

00:04:55.240 --> 00:05:00.650
first column of the change of basis matrix
should be. Pause the video and give yourself

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:09.340
a minute to think about it.

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:13.410
If you weren’t sure how to do this, I explain
it in my video about matrices so go have a

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:17.870
look at that in the description, and in my
playlist linked in the corner. But remember,

00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:24.030
the columns of a matrix just tell you what
happens to the basis vectors. The first column

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:30.950
is what happens to the first basis vector,
here it becomes this. Similarly, the second

00:05:30.950 --> 00:05:37.110
basis vector becomes this, so that’s your
second column.

00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:42.670
This is exactly the matrix we want, because
if Alice puts in the column c d, it comes

00:05:42.670 --> 00:05:47.670
out as this column, just as we calculated
earlier. But there’s one really strange

00:05:47.670 --> 00:05:54.210
thing about this matrix. Usually, a matrix
takes a vector and transforms it into some

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:59.870
other vector. That’s not what’s happening
here. The matrix takes a vector, and gives

00:05:59.870 --> 00:06:06.840
you back the exact same vector, it just changes
how you describe it. That’s something to

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:13.360
keep in mind. But despite this, the change
of basis matrix is very useful.

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:18.480
For example, you can compute it’s inverse,
which has an important property. The inverse

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:24.840
takes a vector that’s in Bob’s basis,
and translates it back to Alice’s basis.

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:33.000
That’s just because that what inverses do,
they undo the original matrix. So once Alice

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:38.930
has figured out what the change of basis matrix
Q is, if she wants to translate a vector Bob

00:06:38.930 --> 00:06:44.800
tells her, to something she understands, she
just applies Q inverse to it.

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:51.639
There’s another reason that these two matrices
are super useful. If Alice now wants to describe

00:06:51.639 --> 00:06:57.060
a whole linear transformation to Bob, not
just a vector, she can use Q and Q inverse

00:06:57.060 --> 00:07:02.380
to do it. Let’s see how this works for the
case when the linear transformation is from

00:07:02.380 --> 00:07:05.570
n dimensions to n dimensions.

00:07:05.570 --> 00:07:10.960
The easiest way to describe a linear transformation
is to write it as a matrix, but the problem

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:17.110
is, the way your matrix looks depends on which
basis you’ve used. This is what her’s

00:07:17.110 --> 00:07:22.110
looks like in her basis. Now, she needs to
translate it to Bob’s basis, which means

00:07:22.110 --> 00:07:25.440
it will look very different from her own.

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:31.221
Here’s how. She wants this matrix to take
a vector written in Bob’s basis and then

00:07:31.221 --> 00:07:37.830
do the linear transformation, but her current
matrix only takes vectors written in her basis.

00:07:37.830 --> 00:07:43.880
So why don’t we just translate Bob’s vector
by applying the Q inverse? This writes Bob’s

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:49.070
vector in her basis, and in this new form,
you can apply her matrix to it.

00:07:49.070 --> 00:07:57.900
OK great. Are we done? Pause the video to
think about whether this matrix, M_A Qinverse

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:03.090
is the linear transform we want in Bob’s
basis. Then choose one of these options in

00:08:03.090 --> 00:08:05.419
the poll.
1 yes

00:08:05.419 --> 00:08:11.760
2 no, but I don't know what to do next
3 She should apply M inverse again

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:23.730
4 She should apply M.
Pause the video now and try work it out.

00:08:23.730 --> 00:08:29.720
The answer is, she’s not quite done, because
the column vector you end up with is in Alice's

00:08:29.720 --> 00:08:37.099
basis now. But that’s no good because Bob
is expecting that his new matrix takes a vector

00:08:37.099 --> 00:08:44.499
written his way to another vector also written
in his basis, unlike this which is in Alice’s

00:08:44.499 --> 00:08:49.779
basis. We have the right vector, it's just
written the wrong way, so we should apply

00:08:49.779 --> 00:08:58.379
Q to it to get it to Bob’s basis. And now
it’s perfect! Overall, what happened is,

00:08:58.379 --> 00:09:04.809
it got translated into Alice’s basis, then
you apply Alice’s matrix, then you translate

00:09:04.809 --> 00:09:15.259
back to Bob’s basis. In other words, Bob’s
matrix is Q M_A Qinverse.

00:09:15.259 --> 00:09:23.259
It’s a really simple concept when you understand
it, but it’s super important, for example

00:09:23.259 --> 00:09:27.110
when you have some x, y, coordinates, and
someone else is using different coordinates

00:09:27.110 --> 00:09:33.480
to you. You’ll also see it crop up lots
in my quantum mechanics videos, and I’ll

00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:39.269
explain the link more once I’ve covered
Dirac notation.

00:09:39.269 --> 00:09:44.269
But before you go, I’d really recommend
you try these 2 simple multiple choice questions

00:09:44.269 --> 00:09:49.240
